The translation hinges on three details of the original that are easy to lose:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10my[0m is a global pre-set to 123.[22m [38;5;10mscanf[0m writes directly into it and [38;5;10mmulti_stage[0m reads it back rather than taking it as a parameter. So when the second conversion fails, [38;5;10my[0m stays 123 and you get [38;5;10mError: x == 1 but y != 2[0m. I modeled this as a [38;5;10mGlobals[0m struct rather than a Rust [38;5;10mstatic mut[0m, keeping it safe while preserving the observable behavior.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10mscanf[0m assigns left to right and stops at the first failure.[22m Input [38;5;10m1 x 3[0m sets only [38;5;10mx[0m; [38;5;10my[0m remains 123 and [38;5;10mz[0m remains 0. I mirrored this with nested [38;5;10mif let[0m over a byte-at-a-time scanner with one byte of pushback, which also reproduces whitespace skipping across newlines.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mEvery error path falls through [38;5;10mgoto fail[0m,[22m so an error prints its own message [3mand[23m [38;5;10mOperation failed[0m. Only the success path returns early, so [38;5;10mOk![0m is never followed by it.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One judgment call worth surfacing: C leaves integer overflow in [38;5;10mscanf[0m undefined. I matched what glibc actually does on x86-64 — convert with [38;5;10mstrtol[0m semantics, saturate at [38;5;10mlong[0m range, then truncate to [38;5;10mint[0m — so [38;5;10m99999999999999999999[0m yields [38;5;10m-1[0m and [38;5;10m4294967296[0m yields [38;5;10m0[0m, same as the C binary. That is platform-specific behavior rather than anything guaranteed by the standard, so a build against a different libc could legitimately differ here.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I also left the [38;5;10mgoto[0m structure as an [38;5;10mif/else if[0m chain rather than a literal jump, since the control flow is equivalent and the print ordering is preserved.[0m[0m
